1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
This lecture will cover challenges computer architects have in measuring and comparing performance of processors.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Comparing performance can be somewhat complex because there are a lot of factors that come into play.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Let's take a look at four airplanes as a starting point.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:24,000
If you had to guess which had the best performance, how would you start?

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Let's look at the columns.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:34,000
The 747 clearly can carry the most amount of passengers while the Douglas has the best range.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,000
And finally, the Concorde flew the fastest.

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:44,000
It turns out we can't just look at one metric as all aspects are important to the performance of an airplane.

9
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Ultimately, we need to agree upon some way to rate each aspect into the overall picture.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,000
So let's get back to processors.

11
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
How do we decide which metrics are the most important?

12
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:07,000
If you're a research scientist trying to simulate complex real-world systems, you probably care the most about floating-point performance.

13
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,000
However, if you're a mobile app developer, you're going to be compiling a lot of code,

14
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:17,000
and integer performance will be key to minimize recompilation time.

15
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Finally, if I'm deploying a cloud service that uses very large databases sharded across many physical machines,

16
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:35,000
I'm going to want very good memory as well as IO latency and bandwidth.

17
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Ultimately, if you're buying a computer and you want the fastest, you need to know about the workloads you care about.

18
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:46,000
And when you're architecting a new processor, you can't run these workloads and measure them.

19
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:53,000
So we need to be able to project performance just based on the organization and features.

20
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,000
One high-order bit is cost.

21
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:02,000
If you don't care about cost, you'll be able to design the fastest processor limited only by physics and processed technology.

22
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:15,000
But in the real world, where you need to be able to sell your processors for a profit, performance per price is going to be crucial to be a success.

23
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,000
In the remaining time, we're going to cover the following four topics.

24
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:33,000
First, we'll cover important metrics of computer performance, including latency and throughput, how to average these metrics, as well as the iron law and Omdahl's law.

25
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:38,000
To define performance, we must consider what is important to whom.

26
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,000
What's important to a normal person using their laptop or mobile device?

27
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Perhaps they care about the time it takes for a program to run.

28
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:55,000
This response time is simply the time difference between the end time and the start time, and we call that response time or latency.

29
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:05,000
However, from the perspective of a computer center or super competing center manager, it's advantageous to maximize the rate of jobs that are successfully being completed.

30
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,000
This metric is called throughput.

31
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:12,000
You've probably dealt with these metrics before in your daily lives.

32
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,000
When playing an online game, your ping time or latency is important so you don't lag.

33
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:26,000
If you're downloading a software update, the bandwidth or data throughput is what you notice the most.

34
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
So how are these metrics related?

35
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Is the throughput of a system always inversely proportional to the response time of a program?

36
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:40,000
It turns out they're inversely proportional only if there's no overlap between the jobs being run.

37
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:46,000
If there's any overlap, then the throughput will be greater than one over the average response time.

38
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Let's consider a lunch buffet with five entrees, and for illustration, let's assume it takes everyone two minutes to dish up that entree to their plate.

39
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:02,000
This puts the total fill rate of five entrees times two minutes each, equal to ten minutes.

40
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:07,000
The throughput, however, is not one plate every ten minutes.

41
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:12,000
When plate one dishes up the red entree, they move to the blue entree.

42
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:18,000
The person behind them, with plate two, can immediately start dishing up the red entree.

43
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,000
This effectively forms what we will call a pipeline as shown in the diagram before.

44
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:28,000
So while each plate takes ten minutes to fill with five entrees,

45
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:33,000
once the buffet line is filled with five people dishing up simultaneously,

46
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:44,000
you can see that we're filling five plates in ten minutes, so our throughput is one plate completion every two minutes.

47
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,000
As an architect, what can we do to improve performance?

48
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Well, we could simply build a processor that can operate at a faster clock.

49
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
This helps both response time and throughput.

50
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:59,000
But with the end of Moore's law and denied scaling, this has gotten harder.

51
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
For this reason, the multi-core era took over.

52
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,000
With additional cores on a processor, one can get more instructions executed per clock,

53
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:11,000
and this may help response time if queuing is reduced.

54
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:19,000
In this class, we're going to focus on processor time, which is time spent running a program.

55
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:22,000
Now let's start comparing performance between machines.

56
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:27,000
We can basically say machine A is N times faster than machine B,

57
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:33,000
if execution time of B divided by execution time of A is N.

58
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:39,000
N is referred to as the speed-up relative to machine B in this case.

59
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:44,000
We can also specify relative performance differences in terms of percent.

60
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:49,000
If it takes 15 seconds to run on machine A and 10 seconds on machine B,

61
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:57,000
then B is 1.5 times faster or 50% faster than B.

62
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Let's go for a deeper understanding of performance.

63
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,000
A processor doesn't know when a program starts or ends.

64
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,000
It only fetches and executes instructions one at a time.

65
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:13,000
However, processors have different stages or cycles that typically take one machine cycle to execute.

66
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:19,000
As a quick example, an older Snapdragon from Qualcomm runs at 1 GHz,

67
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:25,000
which is 1,000 million cycles per second, so the cycle time is 1 nanosecond.

68
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:34,000
In contrast, the latest AMD Ryzen Zen 5 runs at 4.4 GHz or 4.4 billion cycles per second,

69
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:42,000
so the cycle time is 0.227 nanoseconds.

70
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:47,000
All of these metrics are put together to define the iron law of processor performance.

71
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:53,000
The time to execute program can be broken up into three components.

72
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,000
The first is the number of instructions in the program.

73
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,000
This is the code size or dynamic instruction count,

74
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,000
which heavily depends on the simplicity or complexity of the ISA

75
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:09,000
as well as the ability of the compiler to optimize for the architecture.

76
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,000
The second term is the cycles per instruction or CPI.

77
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:15,000
This is purely a function of the implementation,

78
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:19,000
and it's where a computer architect can make trade-offs in the design

79
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
and in how the processor's pieces are built.

80
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:25,000
This will determine whether instructions will execute in a single cycle,

81
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,000
a few cycles, or many cycles.

82
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,000
The final component is the time per cycle,

83
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:34,000
which is the time elapsed during one clock cycle.

84
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:37,000
This is going to be dependent on the designer

85
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:41,000
and could be different if the same ISA is going to target different markets

86
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
with unique implementations of silicon.

87
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
All three of these components can be intertwined.

88
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:50,000
For example, an ISA with very complex instructions

89
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:54,000
may be hard for the designer to execute in fewer cycles.

90
00:07:54,000 --> 00:08:02,000
There is just too much work to do and not enough time within a cycle to do it.

91
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:05,000
Let's go over an example utilizing the iron law.

92
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:11,000
Machine A has a one nanosecond clock period and achieves a CPI of 2.0,

93
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:17,000
while Machine B has a two nanosecond clock period and achieves a CPI of 1.2,

94
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:23,000
which is faster and by how much.

95
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
Since they're both running the same program,

96
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:31,000
we can assume that the total number of instructions executed, N, is unchanged.

97
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:35,000
Taking the product of the CPI and clock period and comparing the two machines,

98
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:42,000
we find that A is 20% faster than B.

99
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:45,000
But what if you have a set of programs you wish to use as benchmarks

100
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,000
and want to compare them across multiple machines?

101
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:52,000
The easiest way to reason about this would be to assume each program is run once

102
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,000
and sum the execution times on each machine.

103
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:02,000
In this example, Machine B is 9.1 times faster than Machine A.

104
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:07,000
A second approach is to take the arithmetic mean, which actually is the same results.

105
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:12,000
In a similar way, it assumes that each program are equally run on a machine,

106
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:16,000
yielding 9.1x speedup for Machine B.

107
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:21,000
If the programs are not run equally, then you'll need to take the weighted arithmetic mean

108
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:27,000
to properly account for how often each program will be run.

109
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:30,000
Dealing with ratios, as we'll see, is different.

110
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:35,000
Let's try to use arithmetic mean for ratios and see why it does not work.

111
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
The top table shows our runtimes.

112
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:41,000
The bottom table normalizes them to Machine A.

113
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:50,000
If we take the arithmetic mean, we'll see that Machine B is 5.05 times slower.

114
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:54,000
Now let's normalize to Machine B and take the arithmetic mean.

115
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:58,000
Now we find that Machine A is 5.05 times slower.

116
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:04,000
How can both machines be 5 times slower than the other?

117
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:09,000
When finding the mean of ratios, we have to use the geometric mean

118
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:12,000
because it's independent of which machine is used as the reference.

119
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:16,000
Now whether or not we normalize to Machine A or to Machine B,

120
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:20,000
the resulting geometric means or geomens are 1,

121
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:28,000
which indicate the performance of the two machines are equivalent.

122
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:34,000
An important point is that the geometric mean of the ratios is not proportional to total runtime.

123
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:38,000
The arithmetic mean assumes that programs are run an equal number of times,

124
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:41,000
so in this case, Machine B is faster.

125
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:45,000
But the geometric mean instead states that both machines are equal

126
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:51,000
and that would only be true if program 1 is run 100 times more often than program 2.

127
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:56,000
We need to be aware of the context in which we're comparing performance for multiple programs

128
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:01,000
to ensure we're comparing apples to apples.

129
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,000
The last topic of this lecture is Amdahl's Law.

130
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:10,000
This was first presented by Jean Amdahl, a 1952 PhD graduate from the University of Wisconsin-Madison.

131
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:17,000
It's a simple analytical model that serves to show how important it is to optimize the common case in your system.

132
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:22,000
Given F, the percent of time a component occupied in your program's runtime,

133
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:32,000
and S, the speedup you apply to it, then this model estimates the resulting speedup of the entire program.

134
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
Let's look at an example.

135
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:39,000
Your boss challenges you to improve the performance of the next generation processor

136
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:49,000
by either 1 improving the ALU by 10% or 2 improving the memory pipeline by 10 times.

137
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:52,000
Since a crucial program is compute intensive,

138
00:11:52,000 --> 00:12:03,000
it spends 95% of the time crunching numbers and 5% of the time waiting for data to transfer via the memory pipeline.

139
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:11,000
Amdahl's Law can help us compare the two options, and it looks like ALU speedup wins despite a modest 10% speedup.

140
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:16,000
Speeding up the memory pipeline 10x didn't have much of an effect.

141
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:20,000
Even though more than 10x may be theoretically impossible,

142
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:27,000
how much faster do we have to make it in order to equal the ALU speedup?

143
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:31,000
Theoretically, let's consider an infinite speedup of the memory pipeline.

144
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:35,000
All requests now take 0 nanoseconds.

145
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:40,000
Amdahl's Law politely reminds us that this crazy optimization barely moves the result.

146
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:47,000
Hyper-optimizing a small, uncommon portion of the program execution is very limiting in the overall benchmark.

147
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:52,000
Working on the common case will always be more important.

